# toolbox, suppressions

Every `# nosec`, `noqa`, `type: ignore` or equivalent in this repository, with
the question that was asked and the answer that was given. **A suppression that
is not here is a defect**, because the register is the only thing standing
between a silenced check and nobody remembering why.

## S-1, bandit's subprocess findings in the test tree

Twenty-four marks, two rules, eleven files, and every one is a test. `B404` for
importing `subprocess`, `B603` for calling it without `shell=True`.

    tests/conftest.py                         # nosec B404    import subprocess
    tests/conftest.py                    ×2   # nosec B603    the flag-contract runner, and the git helper
    tests/test_voice_review.py                # nosec B404    import subprocess
    tests/test_voice_review.py                # nosec B603    the script-runs-as-a-script case
    tests/test_voice_tells.py                 # nosec B404    import subprocess
    tests/test_voice_tells.py                 # nosec B603    the script-runs-as-a-script case
    tests/test_bolt_result_adapter.py         # nosec B404    import subprocess
    tests/test_bolt_result_adapter.py         # nosec B603    running the adapter
    tests/test_conventions.py                 # nosec B404    import subprocess
    tests/test_conventions.py                 # nosec B603    every adapter spawned without site-packages
    tests/test_coverage_adapter.py            # nosec B404    import subprocess
    tests/test_coverage_adapter.py       ×2   # nosec B603    the run helper, and the two-evidence case
    tests/test_gofmt_adapter.py               # nosec B404    import subprocess
    tests/test_gofmt_adapter.py               # nosec B603    the script-runs-as-a-script case
    tests/test_suppression_register.py        # nosec B404    import subprocess
    tests/test_suppression_register.py        # nosec B603    the script-runs-as-a-script case
    tests/test_shell_files.py                 # nosec B404    import subprocess
    tests/test_shell_files.py                 # nosec B603    the script-runs-as-a-script case
    tests/test_tool_versions.py               # nosec B404    import subprocess
    tests/test_tool_versions.py               # nosec B603    the script-runs-as-a-script case
    tests/test_traceability.py                # nosec B404    import subprocess
    tests/test_traceability.py                # nosec B603    the script-runs-as-a-script case

The rows carry the pragma as it is spelled in the source, and `×N` where a file
holds more than one. That is what makes this an index and not a description of
one: the checker reads these rows with the same patterns it scans the source
with, so a row naming no recognised pragma is prose to it.

### Why the marks, and not an exclusion

The Python jig runs bandit twice: once over the tree without its tests, held to
everything, and once over the test tree alone with `--skip B101`. Its comment
says why B404 and B603 are not also skipped there: "deliberate subprocess use is
a design choice worth seeing rather than noise to silence". Marking each site is
what that sentence asks for. Excluding the test tree would have been one line
and would have stopped anyone ever seeing a new one appear.

`conftest.py`'s git helper is there because both voice checks read a real
repository: the wording check asks git for the tracked files, the staged copy of
one and the messages after a start commit. A test of either builds a repository
and commits into it, so the helper runs git the way the checker does.

### Why the subprocess calls exist at all

This is the question the register is for. A checker or adapter is a script bolt
spawns, so its shebang, its imports and where `main()` writes its envelope are
part of the contract, and an in-process call proves none of them. Every `B603`
above is a test running the real script the way bolt runs it.
`tests/conftest.py`'s `script_argv` is where they all go through, and it also
routes them under `coverage run --parallel-mode`, without which a spawned script
reports 0% with a full suite behind it.

Line numbers are deliberately not recorded. They go stale on the next edit and a
stale line number is worse than none, because it sends a reader to the wrong
call. `grep -rn nosec tests/ adapters/ bin/` is the current list and it is one
command.

## S-2, the standard XML parser in the Cobertura adapter

Two marks, one file.

    adapters/python/coverage.py    # nosec B405    import xml.etree.ElementTree
    adapters/python/coverage.py    # nosec B314    ET.parse on the report

`defusedxml` is not a dependency here, so the adapter uses the standard parser.
That is defensible in exactly one circumstance and this is it: the document it
parses was written by the `tests` task in the same jig, seconds earlier, in the
same work directory. It is not a document arriving from anywhere.

This entry retires when either `defusedxml` becomes a declared dependency or the
adapter stops parsing XML. The first waits on `requires:` naming libraries as
well as executables, which anvil's NEXT_STEPS carries.

The module docstring carries the same reasoning at the point of use, which is
deliberate duplication: a reader of the adapter should not have to find this
file to know why the parser is the standard one.

## S-3, duplication between scripts that cannot import each other

Nine marks, one rule, every standalone script here.

    bin/test-traceability.py       # pylint: disable=duplicate-code
    bin/suppression-register.py    # pylint: disable=duplicate-code
    bin/link-toolbox.py            # pylint: disable=duplicate-code
    adapters/common/bolt-result.py # pylint: disable=duplicate-code
    adapters/go/coverage.py        # pylint: disable=duplicate-code
    adapters/go/gofmt.py           # pylint: disable=duplicate-code
    adapters/go/govet.py           # pylint: disable=duplicate-code
    adapters/python/coverage.py    # pylint: disable=duplicate-code
    adapters/lcov/coverage.py      # pylint: disable=duplicate-code

The cause is structural. Every script here is spawned by path, from a directory that is not
a package, and linked one file at a time into each adopter. None can import
another. So anything two of them must both do is written twice, and R0801
reports a different pair each time one is dissolved:

    the coverage adapters' judgement    the two thresholds, the per-file rule,
                                        the statistics, the envelope, `main`
    the checkers' SKIP_DIRS             the directories neither reads
    the adapters' emit                  eight lines writing `output.yaml`

Dissolving them one at a time is the wrong shape of work: three edits, three
justifications, and a fourth pair surfacing behind them.

One of the three is already guarded by a test, and the others should reach the
same standard.
`tests/test_suppression_register.py::test_both_checkers_skip_the_same_directories`
compares the two skip lists and fails if they diverge. That answers R0801's real
concern, silent divergence, with something that can fail instead of a comment.

This entry retires with a shared module under `adapters/common/` and another
under `bin/`, joined to the `common` set so every adopter links them, imported by
path from scripts that currently import nothing but the standard library and
PyYAML. That is a real change: it puts a module behind nine shipped scripts, adds
a link every adopter takes whether or not it runs the task that needs it, and
reaches the Go coverage adapter, which judges no branches and does not fit the
same shape without thought. `NEXT_STEPS.md` carries it.

**The third option must not be taken**: raising `min-similarity-lines` until the
findings stop. That is `docs/LESSONS/a-dead-threshold-is-a-latent-relaxation.md`
exactly, and it would also silence a case R0801 got right: two test files that
had copied a subprocess block, which was dissolved and not marked.

## S-4, bandit's subprocess findings in the checkers themselves

Nine marks, two rules, three checkers. These are not tests: they are the
production path, where a checker runs the tool it is a checker for.

    bin/tool-versions.py                      # nosec B404    import subprocess
    bin/tool-versions.py                      # nosec B603    each required tool, asked for its version
    bin/voice-review.py                       # nosec B404    import subprocess
    bin/voice-tells.py                        # nosec B404    import subprocess
    bin/voice-tells.py                   ×5   # nosec B603    git: ls-files, show, rev-parse, log, diff --cached

Asked and answered on the same terms as S-1: mark each site rather than skip the
two rules for `bin/`, so a new subprocess call in a checker still has to be
justified here.

Every call passes a fixed program and a list of arguments, with no shell and no
string interpolation into a command line. The program is `git` or the tool the
checker exists to run, found on PATH by name. What varies is a path inside the
project being checked, which reaches the tool as one argument, so a file named
`; rm -rf /` is an argument and never a command.

`bin/voice-review.py` imports `subprocess` and calls none itself. `Access.run`
defaults to `subprocess.run` and a test hands in its own, which is how the claude
CLI is asked for a review without a test ever calling a model.

The alternative was to skip B404 and B603 for the production tree, one flag in
the jig. That hides the next one from every adopter, which is the same argument
S-1 makes for the test tree.
